<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · War and march · 1797 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Poland Is Not Yet Lost</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="pl">Mazurek Dąbrowskiego</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A song written in 1797 for Polish soldiers far from home, saying Poland lives on while its people do. It is Poland's national anthem.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Józef Wybicki</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>not known (probably a folk tune adapted by Wybicki)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Polish Legions in Italy; later the Polish nation</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Polish</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Reggio Emilia, Italy</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>written between 16 and 19 July 1797</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem · War and march</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Józef Wybicki (1797; he died in 1822))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>In 1795 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> was partitioned for the third time by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and erased from the map; the partitioning monarchs pledged never to use the name &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>&quot; in their titles. Many, even leaders of the Polish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>, took it as the end of the nation: Hugo Kołłątaj wrote that Poland &quot;no longer belonged to currently extant nations&quot;. Polish patriots turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, then at war with Austria. In 1796 Józef Wybicki, a leading moderate émigré politician, proposed Polish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legions</a> within the French Revolutionary Army and persuaded General Jan Henryk Dąbrowski, a hero of the 1794 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosciuszko-uprising">Kościuszko Uprising</a>, to put the plan to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-directory">French Directory</a>. In January 1797 the Cisalpine Republic accepted Dąbrowski's offer, and a legion was formed to fight Austria under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> and then march from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> to Poland to raise a national uprising.</p><p>Wybicki reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legions</a>' quarters at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reggio-emilia">Reggio Emilia</a> in early July 1797 and wrote the &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> of the Polish Legions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>&quot; between 16 and 19 July. He first sang it at a private meeting of officers in the episcopal palace; its first public performance was most probably at a military parade in the cathedral square on 16 July, and it was played again on 20 July as the Legions marched to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>. Its idea, new in the age of early nationalism, was that a nation could exist without a state of its own as long as its people lived and fought for it. It names military models: Dąbrowski, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>, the 17th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hetman">hetman</a> Stefan Czarniecki and Kościuszko. The melody is a lively mazurka, most probably Wybicki's adaptation of a folk tune popular in the later 18th century; its composer is not known. It was long wrongly credited to Michał Kleofas Ogiński, until his march for the Legions was found in 1938 and proved to be a different piece.</p><p>The song caught on quickly with officers, mostly émigré nobles, and with ordinary soldiers, most of them Galician peasants captured from the Austrian army. Dąbrowski wrote to Wybicki on 29 August 1797 that the soldiers liked it more and more. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legions</a> themselves never marched home: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> used them against uprisings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and later in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>, where war and disease destroyed them. When Dąbrowski and Wybicki arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poznan">Poznań</a> on 6 November 1806, crowds greeted them with the song, and it became one of the most popular patriotic songs of the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>. Its words kept changing: after the Franco-Russian alliance at Tilsit in 1807 the stanza naming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>'s enemy was dropped, and so was the stanza on Kościuszko, who had refused to support <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>.</p><p>Through a century of foreign rule after 1815 it was sung in every national uprising; in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/november-uprising">November Uprising</a> of 1830–1831 it was chanted on the battlefields, and at least 16 new versions were written. Dąbrowski's name was at times replaced by later leaders, from Józef Chłopicki to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jozef-pilsudski">Józef Piłsudski</a> and Władysław Sikorski. The Great Emigration carried it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>. In the partitioned lands it became risky to sing: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> banned Polish patriotic songs in 1850, and between 1873 and 1911 German courts passed 20 sentences for singing this one; under Russian rule public performances often ended with police intervention. Adam Mickiewicz placed it twice in Pan Tadeusz (1834), and in 1842 he explained its opening idea to his students in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> regained independence in 1918 the song became the de facto anthem of the Second Polish Republic and was officially adopted in 1927, ahead of rivals such as &quot;Boże, coś Polskę&quot;, &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warszawianka">Warszawianka</a>&quot; and &quot;Rota&quot;. At the inauguration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> in 1945, to which no Polish delegation had been invited, the pianist Artur Rubinstein opened his concert by protesting its absence and playing the anthem loudly and slowly; President Aleksander Kwaśniewski recalled this in 2005. Today it is one of three national symbols defined by the Polish constitution, played in F major on public occasions in Kazimierz Sikorski's arrangement, and broadcast every midnight on Polish Radio's First Programme.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A song of hope and resolve: the nation survives in its people even when it has no state of its own. It calls the soldiers to fight under Dąbrowski to win back what foreign powers have taken and to march home, holding up past heroes as models and picturing the families waiting for their return. The mood is brisk and confident, carried by a dance rhythm.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Polish</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Polish</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Jeszcze Polska nie zginęła</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Poland has not yet perished</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Kiedy my żyjemy,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>While we are alive,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Co nam obca przemoc wzięła,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>What foreign force has taken from us</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Szablą odbierzemy.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We shall take back with the sabre.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Ref: Marsz, marsz, Dąbrowski,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Ref: March, march, Dąbrowski,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Z ziemi włoskiej do Polski</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>From the Italian land to Poland,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Za twoim przewodem,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Under your leadership</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Złączym się z narodem.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We shall unite with the nation.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Przejdziem Wisłę,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We'll cross the Vistula,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Przejdziem Wartę,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We'll cross the Warta,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Będziem Polakami,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We shall be Poles,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Dał nam przykład Bonaparte,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Bonaparte has given us the example</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Jak zwyciężać mamy.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Of how we are to win.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Ref: Marsz, marsz...</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Ref: March, march...</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Jak Czarniecki do Poznania,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Like Czarniecki to Poznań,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Po szwedzkim zaborze,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>After the Swedish seizure,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Dla ojczyzny ratowania,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To save the fatherland</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Wrócim się przez morze.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We shall return across the sea.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Ref: Marsz, marsz...</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Ref: March, march...</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Już tam ojciec do swej Basi,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Now there a father to his Basia</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Mówi zapłakany -</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Says, in tears —</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Słuchaj jeno, pono nasi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Just listen, it seems ours</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Biją w tarabany.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Are beating the kettledrums.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pl">Ref: Marsz, marsz...</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Ref: March, march...</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Józef Wybicki (1797); the official text. Text from <a href="https://pl.wikisource.org/wiki/Mazurek_D%C4%85browskiego" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (9)</summary><ul><li>line 1.2: 'Kiedy' here means 'while, as long as' (not 'when'); Wybicki's 1797 manuscript has 'Póki' with the same sense. This is the official text of 1927, which differs from the 1797 original in wording and stanzas.</li><li>line 1.4: 'Szablą' — the sabre, the szabla of the Polish cavalry.</li><li>line 2.1: Jan Henryk Dąbrowski, commander of the Polish Legions in Italy (1797), for whom the song was written; 'Ref:' is the text's label and kept.</li><li>line 2.3: 'za twoim przewodem', under (following) your lead or command.</li><li>line 5.1-5.2: Stefan Czarniecki, who fought the Swedes after their invasion of 1655 (the Deluge); 'zabór' is the Swedish seizure (occupation) of the country. Poznań is a city.</li><li>line 5.4: 'Wrócim się przez morze' — 'we shall return across the sea', the Legions' way home from Italy; it also echoes Czarniecki's crossing to Als in 1658 (not said in the text).</li><li>line 7.1: Basia, a pet form of Barbara, the father's daughter.</li><li>line 7.3: 'jeno' (only, just) and 'pono' (it seems, they say) are archaic.</li><li>line 7.4: 'tarabany', drums or kettledrums (an old loanword).</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
